        <div id="pressure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Do Side Sleepers Need Pressure Relief at the Shoulder and Hip?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On your side, the shoulder and hip stick out furthest and carry most of your weight. On a firm surface, that concentrated pressure causes the numb arm, the aching hip and the urge to roll over every hour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sinkage is the whole point</h3>

          <p>
            A good side sleeper mattress lets the shoulder and hip sink until the waist meets the surface. Then the spine runs roughly straight from neck to tailbone. If the shoulder cannot sink, the upper spine bends. If the hips sink too far, the lower spine sags.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why comfort layer thickness matters most for side sleepers. The Helix, Purple and Brooklyn picks each put several inches of soft or adaptive material over their coils, according to their makers and reviewers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How zoned support helps</h3>

          <p>
            Helix says the Midnight Luxe uses firmer coils under the lower back and softer coils under the shoulders. Owners of non-zoned beds commonly report the trade-off this solves: soft enough for the shoulder lets the hips dip, and firm enough for the hips is hard on the shoulder.
          </p>
        </div>

