        <div id="foam-vs-hybrid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Foam or Hybrid: Which Is Better for Side Sleepers?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both work. Each fits a different kind of side sleeper, and the deciding factors are body weight, heat and how much you move.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Memory foam: the deepest contouring</h3>

          <p>
            Memory foam softens with body heat and slowly molds around the shoulder and hip. The TEMPUR-Cloud and Nectar Classic lean on this feel. The downside is a stuck-in feeling, and reviewers note it makes changing position harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            All-foam beds also tend to sleep warmer, because dense foam traps heat. Gel and cooling covers help, with mixed results in owner feedback.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid: support plus bounce</h3>

          <p>
            A hybrid puts foam over pocketed coils. The coils give firmer support under the hips, add bounce and let air move, which is why hybrids suit heavier and hotter side sleepers. They cost more. Under about 230 pounds, if you sleep cool and like the hug, a foam bed is a sound way to save money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hip-pain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Is the Best Mattress for Side Sleepers With Hip Pain?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The sore hip is often the one you lie on. A mattress cannot treat the cause, but it can reduce how hard the surface pushes back on the joint. That usually means more cushioning at the hip, with enough support to keep the pelvis level.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to look for</h3>

          <p>
            Choose a soft or medium feel with a thick comfort layer. The Purple Restore Soft and Helix Midnight Luxe both fit. Purple says its grid collapses under pressure points, and owners commonly describe the hip feeling cradled rather than squeezed.
          </p>

          <p>
            A bed that is too firm is the classic mistake. For a side sleeper, firm concentrates pressure on the bony point of the hip. If your current bed is firm but sound, a soft topper is a cheaper first experiment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small changes that help</h3>

          <p>
            A pillow between the knees stops the top leg twisting the pelvis. No mattress cures hip pain, though. Hip pain that persists for weeks, wakes you up, or comes with stiffness or swelling warrants a doctor or physical therapist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shoulder-back" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Helps Side Sleepers With Shoulder Pain or Back Pain?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sore shoulder wants a softer top layer. A sore back wants even support under the waist. The best mattress for side sleepers with back pain does both, which is why zoned hybrids keep coming up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shoulder pain</h3>

          <p>
            The top 2 to 4 inches do most of the work. Owners commonly report waking with a numb arm on beds that are too firm, a sign the shoulder is not sinking. Pillow height matters too: too low loads the shoulder, too high bends the neck. Lasting shoulder pain, especially with weakness, should be checked by a doctor or physical therapist.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Back pain</h3>

          <p>
            Look for a medium feel with zoned or firmer lumbar support, like the Helix Midnight Luxe. The Leesa and Nectar are slightly firmer options that keep the waist from sagging. Our guide to the best mattress for back pain covers sciatica and firmness in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Persistent back pain, pain down a leg, or numbness are reasons to see a doctor or physical therapist rather than buy a new bed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="combination" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Should Combination Sleepers Look For?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most side sleepers spend part of the night on their back, and some roll onto their stomach. The mattress has to work in each position and let you move between them easily.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Responsiveness over hug</h3>

          <p>
            Combination sleepers do better on a responsive surface that springs back quickly. Hybrids are responsive; traditional memory foam is slow, which frustrates anyone who turns over often.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Leesa Original Hybrid is the pick here, with thinner foam than the Helix and bouncy pocketed coils. Brooklyn Bedding recommends the medium Signature Hybrid for combination sleepers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The firmness middle ground</h3>

          <p>
            For a side and back mix, medium is safest. If you also sleep on your stomach, lean firmer, because a soft bed lets the hips sink and arches the lower back in that position.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pillows-toppers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Can a Pillow or Topper Fix a Mattress That Is Too Firm?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Often, partly. A topper can add the cushioning your shoulder and hip miss on a firm bed in good shape. Nothing on top fixes a mattress that sags or has a body impression.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Toppers</h3>

          <p>
            A 2- to 3-inch memory foam or latex topper is the usual choice for side sleepers. Memory foam hugs deeper; latex is more responsive and cooler. Our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-topper/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold no-underline hover:underline">best mattress toppers</a> compares options for side sleepers and back pain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pillows</h3>

          <p>
            Side sleepers need the highest pillow of any position to fill the gap between ear and shoulder. A softer mattress lets the shoulder sink and reduces the loft you need, so pick the mattress first and the pillow second.
          </p>

          <p>
            A knee pillow keeps the hips stacked. For older side sleepers who want easier entry and exit, our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-for-seniors/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold no-underline hover:underline">best mattress for seniors</a> covers edge support and height.
          </p>
        </div>

