          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Firm Mattress Because It Feels Healthier</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Firm beds are often sold as better for your body, but for side sleepers a firm surface concentrates pressure on the shoulder and hip. Unless you weigh well over 230 pounds or also sleep on your stomach, a medium or softer feel almost always serves a side sleeper better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Your Own Body Weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Firmness labels assume an average sleeper. A 120-pound person feels a medium bed as firm, and a 260-pound person feels it as soft. Read every firmness rating through the lens of your own weight, and use the table above to adjust up or down before you choose.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging a Mattress in the First Week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Your body needs time to adjust to a new surface, and foam softens as it breaks in. Many makers require 21 to 30 nights before a return for exactly this reason. Give a new bed about a month before deciding, unless it is causing real pain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping the Old Pillow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A softer mattress lets the shoulder sink, which changes how much pillow loft you need. A pillow that suited a firm bed may now push the head up at an angle. If your neck feels worse after switching mattresses, try a lower or adjustable pillow before blaming the bed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Topper to Fix a Sagging Bed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A topper adds cushioning, but it cannot restore support. If your current mattress has a dip or a body impression, a topper simply follows the sag, and the hips still drop. Toppers make sense for a firm bed in good shape, not for a worn-out one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

